detailedExplanation.whatItIs:
A function with multiple returns looks like:
func divide(a, b int) (int, error) {
    if b == 0 {
        return 0, errors.New("divide by zero")
    }
    return a / b, nil
}
The caller receives both values: result, err := divide(10, 2).

The pattern looks like:
f, err := os.Open("file.txt")
if err != nil {
    return err
}
defer f.Close()
The error interface is simply:
type error interface {
    Error() string
}

A struct definition and use:
type User struct {
    Name string
    Age  int
}
u := User{Name: "Ada", Age: 30}
fmt.Println(u.Name)
Fields can be any type, including other structs and slices.

A typical defer for cleanup:
f, err := os.Open("file.txt")
if err != nil {
    return err
}
defer f.Close()   // runs when the function returns
// ... use f ...
The Close call is guaranteed even if later code returns early or panics.

Examples of verbs in action:
fmt.Printf("%d items\n", 5)        // 5 items
fmt.Printf("%s\n", "hello")        // hello
fmt.Printf("%v\n", user)            // {Ada 30}
fmt.Printf("%+v\n", user)           // {Name:Ada Age:30}
fmt.Printf("%T\n", user)            // main.User

The empty interface accepts anything:
var x any           // same as interface{}
x = 42
x = "hello"
x = []int{1, 2, 3}
s := x.([]int)      // type assertion to recover the concrete type
